gamestop gamestop gamestop this  struggling video game retailers shares  were worth a few dollars in 2020  by january 28 2021 they peaked at over  450 dollars  game stops stock is surging the frenzy  was seemingly caused by a reddit forum  called wall street bets  it has millions of members who hoovered  up shares in gamestop  in what has been touted as a battle  between wall street and vigilante day  traders  who wanted to stick it to the  establishment investors  sending shock waves through wall street  and even raising alarm at the us  treasury  some trading platforms eventually  restricted trading of gamestop shares  what does this reveal about the u.s  stock market  you've submitted your questions and  alice fullwood our wall street  correspondent  and henry kerr our economics editor have  answered them  why did trading platforms like robin  hood restrict people from buying  gamestop shares  the decision that robin had took to halt  trading of gamestop shares has been  extremely controversial so it is worth  digging into sort of why that decision  was taken  um at the moment the sort of likeliest  theory seems that  they were struggling to cope with the  sort of sheer volume and sheer  amount of interest that users of robin  hood had  in gamestop shares um you know there are  estimates out there that half of all of  robin hood's customers had some exposure  to this stock those pressures may have  sort of contributed to robinhood's  decision to halt uh trading but you know  the bigger picture is that although this  sort of one retail brokerage  um one you know made the decision to  halt trading games top shares  that was because it was sort of uniquely  positioned  um and sort of uniquely exposed to the  dynamics in this you know most other  retail brokerages um with the exception  of a couple others  did manage to keep trading and gamestop  shares open because they weren't as  exposed to the dynamics as robin hood  was  what is short selling and what is a  short squeeze  so short selling is where investors bet  against  a stock and the way they do that is by  borrowing the stock selling it and then  hoping to later buy it back for a lower  price  to re to give the stock back to its  original owner and they then pocket the  difference  in the interim if the price falls so  it's just a standard way that investors  would bet on  a stock going down a short squeeze  is when uh it becomes difficult for the  person who shorted the stock  i.e the person who's borrowed and then  sold it to get it back again and the  reason it's difficult is because the  stock has become a lot more expensive uh  in the meantime  so they get squeezed and because if i'm  a short seller i'm contractually obliged  to give back the stock that i borrowed  i essentially have to go into the market  and buy it however high the price has  gone  the only alternative is to breach my  contract or to go bankrupt  so a short squeeze is just when there's  lots of people who have shorted the  stock  and the price of that stock goes up a  lot and it becomes hard for the shorts  to get out of their trade  and that's precisely what's happened in  the case of gamestop has gamestop  shown there's one rule for wall street  and another for ordinary people  inevitably yes because the  you know hedge funds who operate every  day on wall street and have massive  uh amounts of uh uh cash and have  massive to cash to invest and have all  the best software and platforms and  connections and terminals and all these  things  you're never going to be able to match  that as a retail investor sitting at  home  so it's inevitable there's going to be  an asymmetry there uh the more  controversial edge to that question  is whether or not uh regulators or  the system is in some sense conspiring  against the retail investors  which i think is what people on the  forum would would tell you  now it's true that regulators are much  more likely to take a  paternalistic stance towards retail  investors than they would towards  hedge funds i don't think regulators  really worry that much about  hedge funds uh losing money unless  there's some sort of systemic threat to  the system  whereas historically regulators have  cared quite a lot about the idea that  retail investors  know what they're doing when they get  into markets now of course there are a  lot of people  uh in the current situation who are  buying gamestop shares who say we know  exactly what we're doing and this is all  a ruse  saying that we don't trying to protect  us from ourselves to actually  keep us out of this market which we're  revolutionizing but the thing is it's  got so vile now that of course there  will be people who don't  and i think regulators will be  interested in that it is interesting  um in the context of sort of how much  access retail investors have now though  compared with what they used to have in  the past  you know there's so much direct access  the sort of  the fact that it's frictionless now  whereas you used to have to pay trading  fees  um how much information um is accessible  sort of online and can be shared  you know between sort of regular  investors um  this is um and it can be hard to see  this at the moment sort of  the most um probably the most equal it's  ever been  between wall street and ordinary people  you shouldn't sort of lose sight of the  fact that  actually now is sort of a a period of  sort of  great access of regular investors that  contrasts with  how things used to be it is the case  that short selling  uh in the market generally is something  that aids  price discovery it it helps the market  function and work out what prices should  be  weren't the case that short selling now  always came with the risk that you'd  sort of become an internet meme and have  loads of people come in and buy the  stock to implement a short squeeze  you might see that as a bad thing for  markets for price discovery generally  however because what's happened with  gamestop is so unique  in so far as the shorts positions were  so large relative to the liquidity of  the trading  it's unlikely really that that will be  repeated again but it's interesting that  a lot of what's going on  is uh people writing online that these  short sellers are sort of  you know bad actors for having sold the  stock short  uh especially during a economic downturn  like when we're in  yeah short sellers are you they're easy  to villainize but they  they are not necessarily always the  villains how have the authorities  reacted  should the situation be regulated  there's definitely a sense from some  politicians  and um kind of across the spectrum  really  um that it was not legitimate for for  robin hood to have uh hold the trading  in the way that they did  um i think that that uh case is going to  or that argument is going to struggle a  lot um based on sort of terms of service  and laws as they're currently in place  there's also been this sort of broader  discussion i guess  of whether or not retail investors  should have sort of as much  access and and should be able to trade  sort of all the  the highly leveraged derivatives and  things that they are trading  um that's a debate that sort of is a  turtle of wall street  and eternal with markets regulators what  does gamestop tell us about the state of  the us stock market  is it overheated there's a sense in  which what's going on with gamestop  is an isolated phenomenon and it's  actually small relative to the market at  large  but you could you know the short squeeze  the  uh what's going on with the uh options  trading which sort of  has these momentum effects uh driving  one's stock up very  high it has had spillover effects but in  general i would view the gamestop thing  as an isolated phenomenon  now of course some people say can't you  look at what's going on here which is  a lot of everyday investors sitting at  home punting on the  stock market and doesn't that tell you  something generally about the kind of  economic situation we're in  my take on that would be that the easy  money is a response to the economic  circumstance and high asset prices are a  natural consequence  and it doesn't have to be a bubble for  that to be true but it is the case that  i don't think this could have happened a  year ago  without the amount of liquidity uh in  the system and without the amount  of money in people's bank accounts as a  result of  the stimulus measures in response to the  pandemic  so if you were someone who believed that  the stock market is a massive bubble  this is a sort of string to the bow of  your argument because you can say look  isn't it always the case in history that  stock market bubbles were  accompanied by these kind of mad pockets  of exuberance and isn't that what we're  seeing here  i think i'd argue that what we're really  seeing here is a sort of  new phenomenon about uh the internet  ganging up against the establishment and  using financial markets to do so and  that's not something we've really seen  before there'll be a lot of  um sort of digging into that point uh to  really try and find out sort of how much  um was driven by initially sort of  retail exuberance but what role  other factors played whether it's this  sort of derivatives um uh trading  whether it's the  short squeeze or whether it's sort of  big institutions piling in right now the  sort of story is that oh it's all the  retail investors against the hedge funds  but it may turn out that a lot of hedge  funds were on the same side as the  retail investors all along  and that they were sort of a big factor  in driving that stock as well but i do  think that it's kind of interesting that  we've gone from  um sort of worrying about bubbles in in  companies that that  you know you might think would do well  in the pandemic the big tech stocks um  and now this sort of form has turned its  attention on the sort of really  unloved value companies you know cinema  chains blackberry nokia  um gamestop etc you know all of these  firms  um are sort of the the reverse trade of  the bubble trade almost because they  were they're sort of um their business  models and their sort of  uh evaluations were so depressed  actually before this this happened so i  i do think that it's sort of slightly  different from that  that dynamic it's that's really  interesting point about the  uh hedge funds interest now in the web  forums because of course that  is what you'd expect not only if you're  an investor who wants to know where  markets are going to go  would you be on the lookout for these  sorts of phenomena uh if you were  someone who potentially wanted to  manipulate the market you might try and  start your own you know meme on wall  street bets for one of these stocks  having invested in it  uh in a sort of classic pump and dump  scheme so i would have thought that  after this whole  thing is over there'll be quite a lot of  uh interest  in the form by investors and also  potentially uh  some some bad actors might see it as an  opportunity  to to try and manipulate the market has  gamestop  started a trend will this happen to  other companies shares  i mean i would fully expect that  something similar might happen to other  companies shares  uh insofar as i think the  widespread retail access to financial  markets  are here to stay i think that uh you  know  reddit's here to stay these social media  dynamics are here to stay  the sort of slightly populist dynamics  of what's going on  are going to be around for a while  they're a feature of  how society is at the moment i do think  gamestop was uniquely  uh positioned for this to happen in  terms of as i've said before in terms of  the amount of short interest  and uh the the sort of thin trading in  the underlying shares  uh so i'd be surprised if we saw  anything as remarkable quite as  remarkable as this happen  again but i think that you know these  retail traders are going to be an  ongoing force in the markets yes  i'm alice forward the economist wall  street correspondent you can read more  of my analysis of the game stock story  and its implications by clicking the  link  and please don't forget to subscribe
 

